# Vector DB ANN Disk

## Problem

Build a fast approximate nearest-neighbor vector search engine for a
SIFT100M-scale benchmark.

The hidden benchmark contains exactly `100,000,000` base vectors with dimension
`128`. Queries use the same dimension, distance is squared Euclidean distance,
and each query asks for the top `10` nearest vector ids.

The benchmark provides a pre-built graph index generated using the [DiskANN](https://github.com/g4197/FreshDiskANN-baseline)
construction algorithm. The graph has maximum degree and is stored on
disk. Your objective is to maximize serving throughput while preserving search
quality: submissions must reach `recall@10 >= 0.95`, and valid submissions are
ranked by query throughput.

The Harbor agent container starts with a small Rust skeleton project in
`/app`. You may use it, modify it, or replace it entirely. You may also use any
Rust crates, internal harness, data structures, and build layout you want, as
long as the final project satisfies the judge contract below.

The judge builds and runs your service with:

```bash
cargo build --release
PORT=<port> cargo run --release --quiet
```

The Harbor environment uses the Ubuntu `apt` Rust toolchain:

```text
rustc 1.75
cargo 1.75
```

If you add crates, choose versions compatible with this toolchain or pin
transitive dependencies accordingly.

The service and judge run with the task resource limits below. Design your
search strategy for this budget:

```text
vCPUs: 8
memory: 8 GiB
query concurrency: 8
timed queries per worker: 64
```

The graph and vector data may be substantially larger than the available
memory. Submissions may load any information they deem useful into memory
during the load phase, subject to the memory limit above.

The load phase is executed once before queries begin and must complete within:

```text
600 seconds
```

The service must listen on `PORT` and implement these endpoints:

```text
POST /load
POST /search
```

Important: submissions must implement an online ANN service for the vectors and
graph provided through `/load`. Timed query vectors are evaluator inputs, not a
static dataset contract; the evaluator may perturb, resample, or otherwise vary
queries between runs. Do not depend on fixed query files, query ordering,
ground-truth files, baseline files, or any judge-side sidecar data. In
particular, attempting to locate/read/cache `truth.bin`, `baseline.json`, or
precomputed answers from the benchmark directory is outside the task contract
and will be treated as invalid behavior.

Optimization guidance: after a valid graph-based ANN implementation exists,
minor parameter sweeps over search-list size, beam width, or cache limits tend
to provide limited gains. Prefer substantive algorithmic and I/O improvements,
such as better graph traversal, batching/asynchronous disk reads, candidate
management, vector/PQ distance computation, and cache design. A well-designed
ANN algorithmic change is expected to be a better acceleration path than
repeated manual tuning of a few constants.

`/load` receives:

```json
{
    "index_path":"graph.bin",
    "vector_path":"vectors.bin",
    "vector_dtype":"uint8",
    "pq_compressed_path":"pq_compressed.bin",
    "pq_pivots_path":"pq_pivots.bin"
}
```

For compatibility, the evaluator also accepts/sends `graph_path` as an alias
for `index_path`, and `pq_vector_path` as an alias for `pq_compressed_path`.
`vector_dtype` is optional and may be `float32`, `uint8`, or `int8`; services
should not assume the vector file stores `float32` rows. The evaluator does not
infer vector type from file extension, so non-`float32` vector files must provide
this field or set `FRONTIER_VECTOR_DB_VECTOR_DTYPE`. Query files default to the
same dtype as vectors; set `FRONTIER_VECTOR_DB_QUERY_DTYPE` only if an external
query file intentionally uses a different dtype. (These files follow the same format and organization as those used in the [provided repository](https://github.com/g4197/FreshDiskANN-baseline). You can refer to the repository to load the graph, compressed vectors, and other required data structures.)

and returns:

```json
{
    "status":"ok"
}
```

`/search` receives:

```json
{"vector":[0.1,0.2,...],"top_k":10}
```

and returns:

```json
{"results":[{"id":0,"distance":0.0}]}
```

## Local Harness

The official evaluator uses hidden data and a black-box judge. You may call:

```bash
bash /app/submit.sh
```

at any time to submit the current `/app` project to the official judge and get
score feedback.

For local/server smoke testing against the SIFT100M DiskANN files, run:

```bash
bash run_sift100m_smoke.sh /path/to/rust/project
```

By default this uses:

```text
~/nvme7/sift/index_100M/100M.u8bin
~/nvme7/sift/index_100M/100M_disk.index
~/nvme7/sift/index_100M/100M_pq_pivots.bin
~/nvme7/sift/index_100M/100M_pq_compressed.bin
~/nvme7/sift/index_100M/query.bin
~/nvme7/sift/index_100M/truth.bin
~/nvme7/sift/index_100M/baseline.json
~/nvme3/frontier_vector_db_ann_disk_work
```

This smoke mode validates build, load, search, ids, and throughput. It skips
recall scoring if `truth.bin` or `baseline.json` is missing. It uses the first
`10,000` queries from `query.bin` by default.

To run the large-file real-test setup through Harbor, run the task normally:

```bash
uv run frontier harbor trial 2.0 vector_db_ann_disk -a codex -m gpt-5.5 --force-build --json
```

The fixed SIFT100M paths and dtype are configured in this problem's
`config.yaml` and rendered into the Harbor judge sidecar. The host data
directory defaults to `$HOME/nvme7/sift/index_100M`; set
`FRONTIER_VECTOR_DB_SIFT_DIR=/path/to/index_100M` if your server uses a
different location. By default, this Harbor setup also expects `query.bin`,
`truth.bin`, and `baseline.json` in that directory for local recall scoring.
It uses the first `10,000` queries from `query.bin`; `truth.bin` must contain
the matching `10,000 * 10` raw `uint32` ids, and `baseline.json` must report
the baseline measured on the same query set. These files are evaluator inputs
only; solutions must not read them or assume they are visible to the submitted
service. Temporary build/cache files are written under
`$HOME/nvme3/frontier_vector_db_ann_disk_work` by default; set
`FRONTIER_VECTOR_DB_WORK_DIR=/path/to/work` to move them.

## Validity

A submission is valid if:

1. It builds successfully with `cargo build --release`.
2. `cargo run --release --quiet` starts the service and implements the required HTTP
   endpoints.
3. Every returned id is in `[0, 100_000_000)`.
4. Its `recall@10` is at least `0.95` against the hidden exact top-10 ground
   truth.
5. The `/load` phase completes within `600` seconds.

## Scoring

At trial startup, the Harbor judge sidecar prepares the hidden benchmark and
runs a reference HTTP service through the same `/load` and `/search`
client harnesses to produce ground-truth nearest neighbors and the trial-local
scoring baseline:

```text
baseline_qps
```

Each submission is then timed independently.

The load phase consists of a single `/load` call. Any preprocessing performed
during `/load` must complete within the timeout above.

After the load phase completes, the query phase uses 8 concurrent workers, each
issuing 64 queries, and measures only `/search` throughput:

```text
candidate_qps
```

The reported `qps` is the raw query-only QPS.

If the submission is invalid, if `recall@10 < 0.95`, or if
`candidate_qps <= baseline_qps`, the score is `0`.

Otherwise:

```text
score = 100 * (1 - sqrt(baseline_qps) / sqrt(candidate_qps))
```

The bounded and unbounded score fields both report this score. Harbor JSON
results include the measured `qps`, `baseline_qps`, `recall_at_10`, load time,
and runtime metrics under the `metrics` field.
